<GARY-S> I wondered - I FIGURED THE STUFF ON POSTPONE WOULD ELICIT A RESPONSE 
FROM YOU
<GARY-S> sorry - didn't mean to shout
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> I'm not sure what that would be.
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> They've made [compile] less obsolete is all I've noticed.
<GARY-S> That pleases you - right ?
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> That's fine with me.
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> The problem is that POSTPONE has been claimed to be a 
substitute for both
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> COMPILE and [COMPILE] .
<[Robert] R.BERKEY>
<GARY-S> That's where I expected some comment from you
<GARY-S> reference to state smart
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> As long as it's less than 100% compatible, then it's not a 
substitute.
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> Interestingly, I've heard of a technique that allows 
COMPILE to be
<JAX> What is it that [compile] can do that you can't do with POSTPONE?
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> implemented by native code compilers.
<GARY-S> There's going to be a lot of code the runs now, fail under X3 J14 
without some serious prelude work
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> jax, POSTPONE [COMPILE] is not the same as POSTPONE 
POSTPONE .
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> Mitch makes the interesting comment that COMPILE, does 
everything that POSTPONE
<JAX> No, but [COMPILE] FOOBAR is pretty much the same as either FOOBAR or 
POSTPONE FOOBAR.
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> does.
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> The implementation for COMPILE is done with two passes, one 
to create threaded
<[Robert] R.BERKEY> code, and then it's the threaded code that is translated to 
native code.
